WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.977
The primary implementation pattern is
simple:

2
00:00:03.057 --> 00:00:07.020
build a new persistence update from the
fields approved for this operation.

3
00:00:07.100 --> 00:00:09.886
If displayName is present, copy its
validated value.

4
00:00:09.966 --> 00:00:12.072
If timezone is present, copy that value.

5
00:00:12.152 --> 00:00:15.743
Then pass only the newly constructed
object to the repository or ORM.

6
00:00:15.823 --> 00:00:18.919
The syntax may use conditional statements,
a small mapper,

7
00:00:18.999 --> 00:00:21.291
or carefully written conditional spreads;

8
00:00:21.371 --> 00:00:23.971
the security property is the explicit
field list.

9
00:00:24.051 --> 00:00:28.572
This mapping creates a stable boundary
between API evolution and database evolution.

10
00:00:28.652 --> 00:00:33.110
Adding isAdmin, billingState, or another
sensitive column to the persistence model

11
00:00:33.190 --> 00:00:35.853
does not make it writable through profile
editing.

12
00:00:35.933 --> 00:00:39.586
A developer must modify the request
schema, mapping, authorization,

13
00:00:39.666 --> 00:00:41.957
and tests before the new capability
exists.

14
00:00:42.037 --> 00:00:44.452
That friction is intentional and
reviewable.

15
00:00:44.532 --> 00:00:48.371
Give the mapper a narrow return type so
compile-time review can also catch

16
00:00:48.451 --> 00:00:51.671
accidental persistence fields in
developer-authored code.

17
00:00:51.751 --> 00:00:55.033
Do not derive the allowlist from all keys
in the database model.

18
00:00:55.113 --> 00:00:59.200
A helper that picks request properties
using Object.keys of an entity is still

19
00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:01.200
coupled to persistence capability.

20
00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:04.871
Generic update utilities can be safe only
when the caller provides an

21
00:01:04.951 --> 00:01:09.595
operation-specific allowlist and the
utility preserves presence and nested semantics

22
00:01:09.675 --> 00:01:10.295
correctly.

23
00:01:10.375 --> 00:01:14.461
For important operations, direct mapping
is often clearer than abstraction.

24
00:01:14.541 --> 00:01:17.266
Use the validated object's own properties
as input,

25
00:01:17.346 --> 00:01:20.071
and avoid re-spreading raw request data
afterward.

26
00:01:20.151 --> 00:01:23.742
Keep server-generated values separate:
audit actor, tenant scope,

27
00:01:23.822 --> 00:01:25.742
updated timestamp, workflow state,

28
00:01:25.822 --> 00:01:30.032
and ownership should come from
authenticated context, trusted configuration,

29
00:01:30.112 --> 00:01:31.103
or business logic.

30
00:01:31.183 --> 00:01:33.536
Explicit mapping is not merely data
cleanup.

31
00:01:33.616 --> 00:01:37.703
It is the point where a public request is
converted into the narrow persistence

32
00:01:37.783 --> 00:01:40.260
command the application has chosen to
support.
